      "from": "Order",
      "join_rule": "v_contoso5_sales_line.order_date = v_contoso5_fx_rate.date_day AND v_contoso5_sales_line.currency_code = v_contoso5_fx_rate.to_currency AND v_contoso5_fx_rate.from_currency = 'USD'",
      "level": "business",
      "notes": "THE PREDICATE IS DECLARED, AND THE `1` IS MEASURED \u2014 operator-approved 2026-10-08. It was prose until today, held out of `join_rule` so it would not \"stand as an unmeasured claim\"; the cost of that was larger than the claim. `planner/sql.py` reads an edge with no `join_rule` as a SAME-TABLE edge (\"no physical JOIN needed\"), marks both relations joined and emits NO ON clause \u2014 so the predicate was not merely unverified, it was unreachable, and the `composite_key_guard` bound on the rule can only fire on SQL that mentions `to_currency`, which such a plan never does. An enforcement half with no realization half.\nMEASURED 2026-10-08 against contoso5.duckdb, all 223 974 served lines: the triple returns EXACTLY ONE rate per line \u2014 rows_out 223 974, min 1, max 1, 0 violations, 0 unmatched on every one of the five currencies (USD 113 614, EUR 49 203, CAD 24 250, GBP 22 829, AUD 14 078). That 1:1 is what `cardinality: '1'` now asserts, and it replaces the rule's former `never: join on currency alone` with a number: on currency alone the join returns up to 20 090 rates per line (every day that currency was quoted), on date alone 25 (the 25 ordered pairs quoted that day). The rule's old prose cited \"100 450 rows of it\", which is the relation's SIZE and not any fan-out.\n`from_currency = 'USD'` IS NOT A CHOSEN LITERAL. It is the amounts' declared unit \u2014 net_revenue.yaml#grounding.source.columns.net_amount.offers.aggregate.unit \u2014 so the pin is checkable against a declaration rather than being magic. Confirmed independently 2026-10-08 (acceptance/verification/manual_value_check_2026-10-08.md): net_amount is USD on every line regardless of `currency_code`, which is the currency TRANSACTED IN and not the unit of the figure. This edge therefore converts USD amounts OUT to a local currency on request; it is never the way to \"get USD\", and nothing should multiply net_amount by this rate to obtain one.\nSTILL OWED: `tools/mac_measure_edges.py` matches `a.x = b.y` only (its own refusal text), so this edge stays in the generator's `unmeasurable` list with a new reason and carries no `verified_by` yet \u2014 a ref to a record that does not exist would be the dangling-reference defect. The measurement above is reproducible from the SQL in the commit message.",
